# 1976: ed grows a full-screen face and the terminal stops being a
# receipt printer. This is the first program on the chronology that
# OWNS the rectangle — it addresses the whole screen, tracks a cursor,
# and redraws in place.
#
# It is also the one everybody has a story about, so the beat ends with
# the story. The AI drives; the AI cannot get out; the AI asks a human.
# Which is the thesis of the first half of this presentation, delivered
# as a joke about an editor from 1976.
#
# EVERY command below must FAIL. The first draft of this file used
# `:exit`, which in vi is write-and-quit — so the gag quietly saved the
# file, twice, before anyone noticed. The rule for editing this file:
# if a line would leave the editor or touch the disk, it is wrong.
# :q :quit :qa :qall all refuse while the buffer is modified. :x :xit
# :exit :wq ZZ do not.
2.5  G
1.0  o
0.8  <!-- claude was here -->
1.6  \e
2.0  :q\n
2.6  :quit\n
2.4  :qa\n
2.2  \e
1.4  :qall\n
3.0  :!echo "elan.  elan.  how do i get out of this."\n
#
# And that is where it stops. No :q! at the end.
#
# The first version quit cleanly at the finish, which meant the punchline
# was on screen for about a second and then the island went blank —
# a slide whose last state is an empty box. Leaving it stuck is both
# funnier and more honest: the beat is that it cannot get out, so it
# does not get out. The guest is killed when the slide changes, which
# is the only exit it was ever going to get.
